<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Patras</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1276 CE (approx.) · capital Patras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest barony of the Principality of Achaea, centred on the port of Patras, later sold to its Latin archbishop.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> was organised around 1209, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patrae">Patras</a>, the main port of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, was granted as a barony with 24 knight's fiefs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-aleman">William Aleman</a>, a knight possibly of German or Provencal origin. He fortified the ancient citadel above the town.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The barons held the castle and city, owed service to the prince and shared the town with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> archbishop, who was the senior Catholic prelate in the principality. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patrae">Patras</a> was a commercial centre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, currants and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with a Greek majority under Latin lords.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Over the 13th century the archbishops acquired the baronial rights. Around 1276 the barony passed to the archbishop, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-archbishopric-of-patras">Latin Archbishopric of Patras</a>, a church-ruled state that lasted until 1430.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-patras.jpg" alt="Barony of Patras" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by Constantine · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1276 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Patras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Aleman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Patras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=barony-of-patras" aria-label="Barony of Patras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5221 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barony of Patras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5541 1060L5530 1071M5541 1060L5541 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5530 1071h0M5541 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5541 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5541 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=barony-of-patras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
